<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brescia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brixia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brescia is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Founded as Brixia, it was the chief town of the Cenomani <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> before becoming a Roman town, whose forum and Capitolium temple survive. A free commune in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it was ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1426 to 1797 and became an important centre of arms manufacture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brescia.jpg" alt="Brescia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luca Giarelli · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tramonto_su_Brescia_(Foto_Luca_Giarelli).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
